  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186054802,
      "id": "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313",
      "title": "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "628115e8-48a0-5d7a-86d9-51c0ff7d429c",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054802/assets/bd81d4339b2312a0c28fc14fa4328311f76d7fbbd5aac03ada12be8f2b2e9111.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-1)$ and $(3,-3)$, respectively.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054802/assets/7006b81048139a97fa026a85ee3bc258a7761fbc5e3aae337f46c3e1e1cc9199.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=- \\frac{2}{3}x-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(3,-3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) &=- \\frac{2}{3}(0)-1 \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-3) &=- \\frac{2}{3}(3)-1 \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=- \\frac{2}{3}x-1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y=-1-\\frac{2}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(3,-3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) &=-1-\\frac{2}{3}(0) \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-3) &=-1-\\frac{2}{3}(3) \\\\ -3 &=-3\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-1-\\frac{2}{3}x$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation: $y+\\frac{2}{3}x=-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(3,-3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+\\frac{2}{3}(0) &=-1 \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark \\\\ (-3)+\\frac{2}{3}(3) &=-1 \\\\ -1 &=-1\\,\\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+\\frac{2}{3}x=-1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nSelect $y=- \\frac{2}{3}x-1$, $y=-1-\\frac{2}{3}x$, $y+\\frac{2}{3}x=-1$.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "d6aaf804-c779-54c4-b442-18c0d75c3348",
          ":answer-field/key": "$y=- \\frac{2}{3}x-1$",
